    "evidence_text": "Management of women who have a genetic predisposition for breast cancer.\n\nNo randomized prospective trials have assessed the impact of these strategies specifically in mutation carriers.\n\nClinical and pathologic characteristics of patients with BRCA-positive and BRCA-negative breast cancer.\n\nOBJECTIVE To identify mutation-specific cancer risks for carriers of BRCA1/2.   \n\n\nWe also estimated RHR, the ratio of breast vs ovarian cancer hazard ratios.\n\nEXPOSURES Mutations of BRCA1 or BRCA2.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOMES AND MEASURES Breast and ovarian cancer risks.   \n\n\np53 mutation in breast cancer. Correlation with cell kinetics and cell of origin.\n\nIn these cases, cell kinetic parameters increased from DCIS to IDC.\n\nLinkage Disequilibrium Mapping of       CHEK2: Common Variation and Breast Cancer Risk     \n\nWhether common polymorphisms in this gene influence breast cancer risk remains unknown.\n\nCommon BRCA1 variants and susceptibility to breast and ovarian cancer in the general population.\n\nFUNDING Cancer Research UK and the UK Medical Research Council.\n\nA common variant in BRCA2 is associated with both breast cancer risk and prenatal viability\n\nBRCA2 is thought to be involved in DNA double strand break-repair.\n\nS49C, may be breast cancer susceptibility alleles.",
